"Today more than half of the world's people are Christians or Muslims: Pew Research Center counted 28.8% Christians and 25.6% Muslims in 2020. This lesson tells how both religions began and spread, as history. Where something is a matter of faith, we say “Christians believe” or “Muslims believe”; where it comes from a later tradition, we say “tradition says”; and where it can be checked, we say “the evidence shows.” We study the two together because their stories touch the same lands and the same routes, not to compare them.": "474cab6a114d",
"Today more than half of the world's people are Christians or Muslims: Pew Research Center counted 28.8% Christians and 25.6% Muslims in 2020. This lesson tells how both religions began and spread, as history. Where something is a matter of faith, we say “Christians believe” or “Muslims believe”; where it comes from a later tradition, we say “tradition says”; and where it can be checked, we say “the evidence shows.” We study the two together because their stories touch the same lands and the same routes, not to compare them. Christianity began with Jesus of Nazareth, a Jewish teacher from Galilee. Around 30 CE, possibly in that very year, he was executed by crucifixion in Jerusalem under the Roman governor Pontius Pilate. Christians believe he rose from the dead. His followers, led by apostles such as Peter and Paul, carried his message to Jewish and non-Jewish communities around the eastern Mediterranean, to Antioch, Alexandria, and Rome. The Roman roads and sea routes, and the Greek language that many people shared, helped them travel. For almost three centuries Christians were a small group, and at times they were punished. In Rome, after a great fire in 64 CE, the emperor Nero blamed them and had many killed. Later emperors sometimes ordered persecutions too. Then in 313 the emperor Constantine made the persecution of Christians illegal, and in 380 the emperor Theodosius I made Catholic Christianity the official religion of the empire. By then it had also spread beyond Roman rule, to Armenia and to Aksum in Ethiopia. Later, along the Silk Roads, a Christian mission reached Chang'an in 635. Islam began in the early 600s in Mecca, a trading town in Arabia. Muslims believe that from 610 the angel Gabriel brought God's words to Muhammad, a man of the city, and that these words were gathered as the Quran. Facing opposition, Muhammad and his followers moved to Medina in 622, a journey called the Hijra; the Islamic calendar counts its years from it. By the time Muhammad died in 632, much of Arabia had accepted his leadership. Within about a hundred years, Muslim rule had spread as far west as Spain and as far east as India, and the caliphs ruled from Damascus and then Baghdad. But ruling a land is not the same as converting its people. Historians such as Richard Bulliet argue that in places such as Iran most people became Muslim only gradually, over about three centuries. Islam also spread along trade routes, carried by merchants and scholars, to West Africa, Southeast Asia, and the ports of China, while in northern India it came with conquest. Both religions lived side by side and met in many ways. In Baghdad a Christian scholar translated Greek books for the caliphs, and in Muslim-ruled Córdoba Christians kept their own communities. There were also wars, such as the Crusades from 1095, when armies from western Europe fought to take Jerusalem, and the Ottoman capture of Constantinople in 1453, which ended the Byzantine Empire. Each side remembered these events differently. By 1500, both religions were spoken of in dozens of languages, across Asia, Africa, and Europe.": "ee326d82ba32",
